REV.page({"course":"english2","id":"w02","title":"Adverbs, tense and aspect, passives, adjectives","summary":"How a verb shows time (tense) and the state of an action (aspect), how and why English builds passives, and the finer points of adjectives and adverbs (degree, comment, viewpoint and focus). Knowing how these parts work is what makes sentences accurate.","n":2,"lectures":[{"code":"2.1","title":"Adverbs – a quick review | types, uses & tips for precise communication","video":"QGkHH_dXtfM","seconds":1425},{"code":"2.2","title":"Tenses & aspects in english | mastering verb forms for accurate communication","video":"bvEyqHfI4Lg","seconds":2251},{"code":"2.3","title":"Structure & functions of passives in english | active vs passive voice made simple","video":"cQ-wetyKv3U","seconds":2436},{"code":"2.4","title":"Some important concepts in adjectives | advanced usage, order & identification tips","video":"hkiHY0chmvk","seconds":1832},{"code":"2.5","title":"Degree adverbs | usage, meanings & tips for precise english","video":"uauQ5eO0Asg","seconds":1009},{"code":"2.6","title":"Adverbs- comment, viewpoint & focus | advanced types for clear and expressive communication","video":"Jyv6b3LU_pE","seconds":1215}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"adverbs-a-quick-review","level":2,"title":"Adverbs: a quick review"},{"id":"adverb-clauses-and-adverb-phrases","level":3,"title":"Adverb clauses and adverb phrases"},{"id":"six-types-of-adverbs","level":3,"title":"Six types of adverbs"},{"id":"tenses-and-aspects","level":2,"title":"Tenses and aspects"},{"id":"three-tenses","level":3,"title":"Three tenses"},{"id":"four-aspects","level":3,"title":"Four aspects"},{"id":"all-twelve-forms","level":3,"title":"All twelve forms"},{"id":"regular-and-irregular-verb-forms","level":3,"title":"Regular and irregular verb forms"},{"id":"passives-how-they-are-built","level":2,"title":"Passives: how they are built"},{"id":"grammatical-roles-and-thematic-roles","level":3,"title":"Grammatical roles and thematic roles"},{"id":"only-transitive-verbs-have-a-passive","level":3,"title":"Only transitive verbs have a passive"},{"id":"passives-with-two-objects","level":3,"title":"Passives with two objects"},{"id":"passives-in-any-tense","level":3,"title":"Passives in any tense"},{"id":"why-we-use-the-passive","level":2,"title":"Why we use the passive"},{"id":"adjectives-some-important-concepts","level":2,"title":"Adjectives: some important concepts"},{"id":"what-adjectives-tell-us","level":3,"title":"What adjectives tell us"},{"id":"spotting-adjectives-by-their-endings","level":3,"title":"Spotting adjectives by their endings"},{"id":"nouns-that-act-as-adjectives","level":3,"title":"Nouns that act as adjectives"},{"id":"attributive-and-predicative-adjectives","level":3,"title":"Attributive and predicative adjectives"},{"id":"same-adjective-different-meaning","level":3,"title":"Same adjective, different meaning"},{"id":"where-adjectives-sit-in-a-phrase","level":3,"title":"Where adjectives sit in a phrase"},{"id":"articles-and-degree-adverbs-before-an-adjective","level":3,"title":"Articles and degree adverbs before an adjective"},{"id":"degree-adverbs","level":2,"title":"Degree adverbs"},{"id":"very-and-too","level":3,"title":"Very and too"},{"id":"very-and-very-much","level":3,"title":"Very and very much"},{"id":"gradable-and-non-gradable-adjectives","level":3,"title":"Gradable and non-gradable adjectives"},{"id":"quite-two-meanings","level":3,"title":"Quite: two meanings"},{"id":"comment-viewpoint-and-focus-adverbs","level":2,"title":"Comment, viewpoint and focus adverbs"},{"id":"comment-adverbs","level":3,"title":"Comment adverbs"},{"id":"viewpoint-adverbs","level":3,"title":"Viewpoint adverbs"},{"id":"focus-adverbs-only-even-alone","level":3,"title":"Focus adverbs: only, even, alone"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>adverb</strong> adds information to a verb, an adjective or another adverb. Ask a question to find its type: <em>how?</em> (manner), <em>how much?</em> (degree), <em>where?</em> (place), <em>when / how often?</em> (time). Sentence adverbs cover the whole sentence; conjunctive adverbs (<em>however, consequently</em>) link two sentences.</li>\n<li><strong>Tense</strong> tells <em>when</em> (present, past, future). <strong>Aspect</strong> tells the <em>state</em> of the action: indefinite, continuous, perfect, perfect continuous. 3 tenses × 4 aspects = 12 verb forms.</li>\n<li><strong>Passive</strong> = a form of <em>be</em> (or <em>get</em>) + the past participle (V3) of a transitive verb: <em>Deepa wrote a novel</em> becomes <em>A novel was written (by Deepa)</em>. We use it when the doer is unknown, unimportant, obvious or better hidden.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjectives</strong> go before a noun (<em>attributive</em>) or after a linking verb (<em>predicative</em>); a few change meaning with position (<em>her late husband</em> / <em>always late</em>). Endings such as <em>-ful, -less, -ous, -ive</em> and positions such as “between <em>the</em> and a noun” help you spot them.</li>\n<li><strong>Degree adverbs</strong> set how strong a word is: <em>very</em> (a high degree) vs <em>too</em> (more than needed); <em>very</em> + adjective but <em>very much</em> + verb; <em>extremely</em> + gradable, <em>absolutely</em> + non-gradable.</li>\n<li><strong>Comment</strong> adverbs give the speaker’s view (<em>frankly, obviously</em>), <strong>viewpoint</strong> adverbs give the angle (<em>financially, technically</em>), <strong>focus</strong> adverbs point at one part (<em>only, even, alone</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"adverbs-a-quick-review\">Adverbs: a quick review</h2>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Grammar words this page uses</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Noun</strong>: a name of a person, place or thing (<em>Maya, college, book</em>). <strong>Verb</strong>: an action or state (<em>run, write, is</em>). <strong>Adjective</strong>: a word that describes a noun (<em>big, red</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Subject</strong>: who or what the sentence is about, usually the doer (<em><strong>Maya</strong> runs</em>). <strong>Predicate</strong>: the rest of the sentence, built around the verb (<em>Maya <strong>runs very fast</strong></em>). <strong>Object</strong>: the noun that receives the action (<em>Deepa wrote <strong>a novel</strong></em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Clause</strong>: a group of words with its own subject and verb (<em>he was tired</em>). <strong>Phrase</strong>: a group of words without its own subject and verb (<em>without any reason</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Modify</strong>: add information to, describe. On this page, → means “becomes”.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The word <em>adverb</em> says what it does: it <strong>adds to the verb</strong>. “Maya runs” is a full sentence. “Maya runs <strong>very fast</strong>” also tells us <em>how</em> she runs. Adverbs answer questions about the action: how, how much, where, when, how often.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>adverb</strong> is a word or a group of words that modifies a <strong>verb</strong>, an <strong>adjective</strong> or another <strong>adverb</strong>. It tells <strong>when, where and how</strong> an action is done, or the <strong>quality or degree</strong> of the action. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture’s examples (the adverb is in bold):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shelly is <strong>always</strong> ready for help.</li>\n<li>He loved her <strong>very much</strong>.</li>\n<li>Time is running out <strong>fast</strong>. (<em>fast</em> adds to the verb <em>run</em>)</li>\n<li>I gave that <strong>willingly</strong>. (<em>willingly</em> adds to the verb <em>gave</em>)</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"adverb-clauses-and-adverb-phrases\">Adverb clauses and adverb phrases</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb clause and adverb phrase</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Adverb clauses</strong> and <strong>adverb phrases</strong> are clauses and phrases that modify the verbs, adjectives or other adverbs in a sentence. They do the job of a one-word adverb, with more words. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Why</th>\n<th>Type</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>He carried the load <strong>until he was tired</strong>.</td>\n<td>own subject (<em>he</em>) and verb (<em>was</em>)</td>\n<td>adverb clause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maya arrived <strong>carrying her suitcases with two hands</strong>.</td>\n<td>no subject of its own; tells us about <em>arrived</em></td>\n<td>adverb phrase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You are irritated <strong>without any reason</strong>.</td>\n<td>no subject of its own; tells us about <em>irritated</em></td>\n<td>adverb phrase</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"six-types-of-adverbs\">Six types of adverbs</h3>\n<p>The lecture sorts adverbs by the question they answer. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Question it answers</th>\n<th>Example from the lecture</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Manner</td>\n<td>How?</td>\n<td>Hold it <strong>carefully</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Degree</td>\n<td>How much?</td>\n<td>Jessy <strong>completely</strong> forgot about her appointment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Place / direction</td>\n<td>Where? In what direction?</td>\n<td>I went <strong>through the jungle</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time / frequency</td>\n<td>When? How often?</td>\n<td>He <strong>always</strong> gets a good result.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sentence adverb</td>\n<td>covers the whole sentence</td>\n<td><strong>Hopefully</strong>, I will finish the assignment.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conjunctive adverb</td>\n<td>links two sentences</td>\n<td>…little rain; <strong>consequently</strong>, we did not have a good harvest.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb of manner</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Shows the <strong>manner</strong> (the way, approach or process) of the action. It answers <strong>“How is the action done?”</strong> Common ones: <em>beautifully, equally, thankfully, carefully, handily, quickly, coldly, hotly, resentfully, earnestly, nicely, tirelessly</em>. They usually end in <strong>-ly</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>We should distribute the workload <strong>equally</strong>.</li>\n<li>Hold it <strong>carefully</strong>.</li>\n<li>He is improving <strong>slowly</strong>.</li>\n<li>Maya runs very <strong>fast</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb of degree</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Shows the <strong>degree</strong> or <strong>level</strong> of the action. It answers <strong>“How much is the action done?”</strong> Common ones: <em>completely, nearly, entirely, less, mildly, most, thoroughly, somewhat, excessively, much</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jessy <strong>completely</strong> forgot about her appointment.</li>\n<li>The policeman examined the documents <strong>thoroughly</strong>.</li>\n<li>She was <strong>so</strong> excited about the new place.</li>\n<li>I <strong>hardly</strong> go to the theatre. (= I almost never go)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">An -ly ending does not tell you the type</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Completely</em> and <em>thoroughly</em> end in -ly, like manner adverbs, but they answer “how much?”, so they are adverbs of <strong>degree</strong>. Ask the question; do not go by the ending alone. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb of place or direction</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Shows <strong>where</strong> the action happens, or in <strong>what direction</strong>. Common ones: <em>across, over, under, in, out, through, backward, there, around, here, sideways, upstairs, in the park, in the field, in that place</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>I went <strong>through the jungle</strong>.</li>\n<li>He works <strong>in a bank</strong>.</li>\n<li>Maya is going <strong>to college</strong>.</li>\n<li>We are staying <strong>at a cottage</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The bold parts above begin with a preposition (<em>through, in, to, at</em>): they are <strong>prepositional phrases</strong>, and here they do the job of an adverb.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adverb of time or frequency</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Shows <strong>when</strong> the action happens, or <strong>how often</strong>. Common ones: <em>always, never, often, eventually, now, frequently, occasionally, once, forever, seldom, before</em>, and days and times (<em>Sunday, Monday, 10 AM, 12 PM</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>I arrived at the airport <strong>a little late yesterday</strong>.</li>\n<li>He <strong>always</strong> gets a good result.</li>\n<li>The PM will leave for America <strong>on Monday</strong>.</li>\n<li>I go to the theatre <strong>occasionally</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentence adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An adverb that shows the <strong>attitude or point of view of the speaker or writer</strong> and refers to the <strong>whole sentence</strong>. It usually comes <strong>at the beginning</strong>, followed by a comma. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hopefully</strong>, I will finish the assignment.</li>\n<li><strong>Apparently</strong>, the days are getting hotter.</li>\n<li><strong>Certainly</strong>, you did not consider asking for my permission.</li>\n<li><strong>Actually</strong>, I think the meeting is on Wednesday, not Thursday.</li>\n<li><strong>Obviously</strong>, we can’t tell you the result but we can give you an indication.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture says these adverbs take the whole sentence “in their scope”. The <strong>scope</strong> of a word is the part of the sentence its meaning covers: <em>hopefully</em> colours the whole idea “I will finish the assignment”. Moving a word to the front like this is called <strong>fronting</strong>.</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Conjunctive adverb (connector)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An adverb that <strong>connects</strong> two phrases or two independent clauses (two full sentences). It shows the <strong>move from one idea to the next</strong> and the <strong>relationship</strong> between them, such as contrast or result. Conjunctive adverbs are also called <strong>connectors</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The train started very late; <strong>nonetheless</strong>, it arrived at the destination on time. (contrast: a late start, yet on time)</li>\n<li>We are still not sure, <strong>however</strong>, if the opportunity will come.</li>\n<li>Last year there was little rain; <strong>consequently</strong>, we did not have a good harvest. (result)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Each part could stand alone as a sentence (“The train started very late.” “It arrived on time.”). To put two such sentences together we need a linker, like <em>and</em> in <em>I reached home, and I changed my clothes</em>. A conjunctive adverb links them <strong>and</strong> shows how the two ideas relate. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QGkHH_dXtfM&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Punctuation with connectors</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slides join these sentences with commas. In careful writing, put a <strong>semicolon</strong> (;) or a full stop before the connector, as above. (A writing tip, not from the lecture.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Find the type with one question</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ask: <em>How?</em> (manner) <em>How much?</em> (degree) <em>Where?</em> (place) <em>When, or how often?</em> (time/frequency). If it stands at the front with a comma and comments on the whole sentence, it is a sentence adverb. If it joins two full sentences, it is a conjunctive adverb.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"tenses-and-aspects\">Tenses and aspects</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A verb names an action or event: <em>play, see, type</em>. Every action happens at some <strong>time</strong> (now, before, later) and is in some <strong>state</strong> (a habit, going on, finished). English shows the time with <strong>tense</strong> and the state with <strong>aspect</strong>, both through the <strong>form of the verb</strong> and its helpers. The lecturer’s point: understand how these markers work, and you gain accuracy; accuracy builds confidence in speaking. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tense</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Tense</strong> is the information about the <strong>time</strong> of an action, event or state. English has three: <strong>present</strong> (now), <strong>past</strong> (a while ago) and <strong>future</strong> (sometime from now). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Aspect</strong> describes the <strong>state of the event</strong>: is it a habit, is it going on, is it complete? English has four: <strong>indefinite (habitual)</strong>, <strong>continuous (progressive)</strong>, <strong>perfect (perfective)</strong> and <strong>perfect continuous</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Auxiliary verb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>helping verb</strong> that stands before the main verb: <em>am, is, are, was, were, will, shall, have, has, had, be, been</em>. Auxiliaries are the <strong>markers of tense</strong>: they carry the time information. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"three-tenses\">Three tenses</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tense</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n<th>Marker</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present</td>\n<td>now</td>\n<td>Ramu <strong>is</strong> in Chennai.</td>\n<td><em>is</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past</td>\n<td>a while ago</td>\n<td>Ramu <strong>was</strong> in Chennai.</td>\n<td><em>was</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future</td>\n<td>sometime from now</td>\n<td>Ramu <strong>will come</strong> to Chennai.</td>\n<td><em>will</em> + a verb</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Is</em> and <em>was</em> are both singular, because <em>Ramu</em> is singular. The only difference between the first two sentences is the tense.</li>\n<li>The future marker <em>will</em> needs another verb after it: “Ramu will <strong>come</strong> to Chennai” or “Ramu will <strong>be</strong> in Chennai”, never “Ramu will to Chennai”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"four-aspects\">Four aspects</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Indefinite (habitual) aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The action is a <strong>habit</strong> or a settled fact, with no limit in time. <em>I see a car in front of that building</em> means I often see it there. <em>I live in Chennai</em> means living in Chennai is my settled state. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present indefinite: I <strong>see</strong> a car in front of that building.</li>\n<li>Past indefinite: I <strong>saw</strong> a car in front of that building.</li>\n<li>Future indefinite: I <strong>will go</strong> home now. / We <strong>shall meet</strong> tomorrow.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>What the markers show (the lecture’s analysis): <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Present:</strong> <em>I live, you live, they live</em>: the verb has <strong>no marker</strong> at all (the lecture writes ∅, “zero”). Only with <em>he, she</em> or <em>it</em> does an <strong>-s</strong> appear: <em>he lives</em>. Because the -s comes only with a third person singular subject, it is an <strong>agreement</strong> marker (it agrees with the subject), not a tense or aspect marker.</li>\n<li><strong>Past:</strong> <em>see</em> → <em>saw</em>. The verb changes form, so the past tense is marked. But <em>I saw, we saw, you saw, they saw</em> are all the same: no agreement marker and no aspect marker.</li>\n<li><strong>Future:</strong> <em>will</em> or <em>shall</em> marks the future.</li>\n<li>The same slide has <em>I am tired</em>. The lecture calls it “just a simple present tense marking”: there is no action in it to describe.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Continuous (progressive) aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The action is <strong>in progress, underway</strong>, at the moment we talk about. The marker is <strong>-ing</strong> on the main verb, with a form of <strong>be</strong> before it (<em>am/is/are, was/were, will be</em>). The <em>be</em> form carries the tense and agrees in number with the subject (<em>the girls are</em>, <em>I am</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present continuous: The girls <strong>are playing</strong> in the ground. / I <strong>am typing</strong> a mail now.</li>\n<li>Past continuous: The girls <strong>were playing</strong> in the ground.</li>\n<li>Future continuous: The girls <strong>will be playing</strong> in the ground.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecture asks you to notice how much the future continuous says: an action that has not started yet, but will be going on at some point in the future. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Perfect (perfective) aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The event is <strong>complete</strong> (“perfected”). This is not the same as past: past is about <em>time</em>; perfect is about the <em>state</em> of being complete. The marker is <strong>have/has/had</strong> + the past participle (for regular verbs, the verb + <strong>-ed</strong>: <em>played, typed</em>). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present perfect: The girls <strong>have played</strong> effectively. / I <strong>have typed</strong> a mail now.</li>\n<li>Past perfect: The girls <strong>had played</strong> effectively. / I <strong>had typed</strong> a mail.</li>\n<li>Future perfect: The girls <strong>will have played</strong> nicely. / I <strong>will have typed</strong> a mail.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Has</em> goes with a singular subject, <em>have</em> with a plural one (<em>the girls have</em>). <em>Had</em> is the same for both (<em>he had played</em>, <em>the girls had played</em>): it changes only for tense. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></li>\n<li>Meaning: present perfect = completed <strong>recently</strong>; past perfect = completed a little <strong>further back</strong>, beyond the present; future perfect = completion is <strong>expected in the future</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Perfect continuous aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Completion in continuity</strong>: an action that has been going on and reaches a point of completion. The lecture calls it the most complex state. Pattern: <strong>have/has/had/will have + been + verb-ing</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Present perfect continuous: The girls <strong>have been playing</strong> effectively. / I <strong>have been typing</strong> a mail now.</li>\n<li>Past perfect continuous: The girls <strong>had been playing</strong> effectively. / I <strong>had been typing</strong> a mail.</li>\n<li>Future perfect continuous: The girls <strong>will have been playing</strong> nicely. / I <strong>will have been typing</strong> a mail.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>In <em>had been playing</em>, the lecture reads three markers: <strong>had</strong> = past tense, <strong>been</strong> = perfect, <strong>-ing</strong> = continuous. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"all-twelve-forms\">All twelve forms</h3>\n<p>V3 means the <strong>past participle</strong>, the “third form” of the verb (<em>played, written</em>); see the verb tables below.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Verb group</th>\n<th>The lecture’s sentence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present indefinite</td>\n<td>base verb (+ -s with <em>he/she/it</em>)</td>\n<td>I <strong>see</strong> a car in front of that building.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past indefinite</td>\n<td>past form</td>\n<td>I <strong>saw</strong> a car in front of that building.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future indefinite</td>\n<td>will / shall + base verb</td>\n<td>We <strong>shall meet</strong> tomorrow.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present continuous</td>\n<td>am / is / are + verb-ing</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>are playing</strong> in the ground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past continuous</td>\n<td>was / were + verb-ing</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>were playing</strong> in the ground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future continuous</td>\n<td>will be + verb-ing</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>will be playing</strong> in the ground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect</td>\n<td>has / have + V3</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>have played</strong> effectively.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect</td>\n<td>had + V3</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>had played</strong> effectively.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect</td>\n<td>will have + V3</td>\n<td>I <strong>will have typed</strong> a mail.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect continuous</td>\n<td>has / have been + verb-ing</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>have been playing</strong> effectively.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect continuous</td>\n<td>had been + verb-ing</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>had been playing</strong> effectively.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect continuous</td>\n<td>will have been + verb-ing</td>\n<td>The girls <strong>will have been playing</strong> nicely.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The same grid with one verb, <em>play</em>, for quick review:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Aspect</th>\n<th>Present</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n<th>Future</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Indefinite</td>\n<td>play / plays</td>\n<td>played</td>\n<td>will play</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous</td>\n<td>am / is / are playing</td>\n<td>was / were playing</td>\n<td>will be playing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perfect</td>\n<td>has / have played</td>\n<td>had played</td>\n<td>will have played</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Perfect continuous</td>\n<td>has / have been playing</td>\n<td>had been playing</td>\n<td>will have been playing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Read a verb group from left to right</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>first</strong> helper carries the <strong>tense</strong>: <em>am/is/are/has/have</em> = present, <em>was/were/had</em> = past, <em>will/shall</em> = future. With no helper, the verb itself shows it (<em>see / saw</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>have/has/had + V3</strong>: perfect.</li>\n<li><strong>be + verb-ing</strong>: continuous.</li>\n<li><strong>have + been + verb-ing</strong>: perfect continuous.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: <em>will have been playing</em> = <strong>will</strong> (future) + <strong>have been</strong> (perfect) + <strong>playing</strong> (continuous), so it is the <strong>future perfect continuous</strong>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Tense is not aspect</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Tense and aspect come together, but they do different jobs: tense = <strong>time</strong>, aspect = <strong>state of the event</strong>. Tense is more general and can come without aspect (<em>I am tired</em>). Aspect needs an event, so there is <strong>no aspect without a verb</strong>. And perfect does not mean past: <em>I have typed a mail</em> is <strong>present</strong> tense with perfect aspect. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1770s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 29:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"regular-and-irregular-verb-forms\">Regular and irregular verb forms</h3>\n<p>Each verb has three main forms: the <strong>present</strong> (base) form, the <strong>past</strong> form and the <strong>past participle</strong> (V3). The past participle is the form used after <em>have</em> (<em>has written</em>) and after <em>be</em> in passives (<em>was written</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Regular and irregular verbs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Regular verbs</strong> make the past and the past participle by adding <strong>-d</strong> or <strong>-ed</strong> to the base verb. <strong>Irregular verbs</strong> make them in other ways: a change inside the word, no change at all, or a completely new word. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 30:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Present</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>act</td>\n<td>acted</td>\n<td>acted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>live</td>\n<td>lived</td>\n<td>lived</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hope</td>\n<td>hoped</td>\n<td>hoped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>am</td>\n<td>was</td>\n<td>been</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>are</td>\n<td>were</td>\n<td>been</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bear</td>\n<td>bore</td>\n<td>borne</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The first three are regular; the last three are irregular. The lecture’s list of irregular verbs: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=2130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 35:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Present</th>\n<th>Past</th>\n<th>Past participle</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>become</td>\n<td>became</td>\n<td>become</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>begin</td>\n<td>began</td>\n<td>begun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>come</td>\n<td>came</td>\n<td>come</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cut</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n<td>cut</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dream</td>\n<td>dreamed / dreamt</td>\n<td>dreamed / dreamt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>go</td>\n<td>went</td>\n<td>gone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>lend</td>\n<td>lent</td>\n<td>lent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rise</td>\n<td>rose</td>\n<td>risen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sew</td>\n<td>sewed</td>\n<td>sewn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shine</td>\n<td>shone / shined</td>\n<td>shone / shined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>spin</td>\n<td>span / spun</td>\n<td>spun</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>strike</td>\n<td>struck</td>\n<td>struck / stricken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>wake</td>\n<td>woke / waked</td>\n<td>woke / waked / woken</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>wear</td>\n<td>wore</td>\n<td>worn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>write</td>\n<td>wrote</td>\n<td>written</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li>Three kinds of irregular change: <strong>inside the word</strong> (<em>begin, began</em>; <em>come, came</em>), <strong>no change</strong> (<em>cut, cut</em>), and a <strong>completely new word</strong> (<em>go, went</em>). A completely new word is called <strong>suppletion</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a></li>\n<li>The <strong>-ed</strong> form does two jobs: it marks the <strong>past tense</strong> (<em>I acted</em>) and it is also the <strong>past participle</strong> (<em>I have acted</em>). The lecture stresses that these are two different things, even when they look the same. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=1860s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:00</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Learning by unlearning</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A child first learns the regular pattern (<em>live, lived</em>; <em>hope, hoped</em>) and then uses it everywhere, saying <em>seed</em> or <em>sawed</em> for the past of <em>see</em>. Slowly she drops the extra -ed and goes back to <em>saw</em>, because the past is already inside the irregular form. Grown-ups no longer learn this way, so they must study the irregular forms as a list. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The lecture’s practice</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Write 20 simple sentences, one per line (from a newspaper or from your head). Check the tense marking in each. Underline any sentence you cannot explain, and check it again with what this lecture teaches. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a></li>\n<li>Take a few regular and a few irregular verbs and put each one into all <strong>3 tenses × 4 aspects</strong>. The lecturer says this helps “dramatically”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a></li>\n<li>Think about the lecture’s closing questions: How are tense and aspect different (answered in Check yourself)? How do they add to what we want to say? How does the verb, a word with its own meaning (a <em>lexical</em> category), relate to tense and aspect, the grammar markers (<em>functional</em> categories)? <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bvEyqHfI4Lg&amp;t=2190s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:30</a></li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"passives-how-they-are-built\">Passives: how they are built</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In daily talk, a “passive” person is not involved; they do not act. A <strong>passive sentence</strong> is similar: its subject does not do the action, it <strong>receives</strong> it. The doer (the <strong>agent</strong>) is moved out of the way: it is hidden, or added at the end with <em>by</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Active and passive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Passive</strong> is a grammatical category that applies to <strong>verbs</strong>. There are two types of sentences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>active</strong>: the subject <strong>does</strong> the action (<em>Deepa wrote a novel</em>);</li>\n<li><strong>passive</strong>: the subject <strong>receives</strong> the action (<em>A novel was written by Deepa</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In an active sentence, the verb carries the <strong>inflection</strong>, the change of form that shows grammar: tense and agreement. In <em>Ravi reads a book</em>, <em>reads</em> shows present tense and agrees with the singular <em>Ravi</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Two more terms. The <strong>direct object</strong> answers “verb + what?” (<em>Deepa wrote what? A novel.</em>). A <strong>noun phrase</strong> (NP) is a noun with its small words (<em>a novel, the girls, my brother</em>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Canonical passive</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Canonical</strong> means the standard, basic pattern. In a passive, a form of the verb <strong>be</strong> (or sometimes <strong>get</strong>) is used as an auxiliary together with the <strong>past participle (V3)</strong> of a <strong>transitive</strong> verb: <strong>be + V3</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Deepa wrote a novel → A novel was written by Deepa (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>Start with the active: <strong>Deepa</strong> (subject, the doer) <strong>wrote</strong> (verb, past) <strong>a novel</strong> (direct object).</li>\n<li>Recast the verb as passive: <em>wrote</em> → <strong>was written</strong>, that is, <em>be</em> in the same tense (<em>was</em>, past) + V3 (<em>written</em>).</li>\n<li><em>Written</em> is now only a participle. Like an adjective (<em>a <strong>broken</strong> chair</em>), it has lost the powers of a verb: no tense, no agreement, and it cannot take an object. All the tense and agreement now sit on <em>was</em>.</li>\n<li>So the object <em>a novel</em> is free. The subject position is empty, because the agent has been pushed out. English needs a subject, so <em>a novel</em> moves into it: <strong>A novel was written</strong>.</li>\n<li>To show the agent, insert the preposition <strong>by</strong>: <strong>by Deepa</strong>. This part is optional.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> A novel was written (by Deepa).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">English needs a subject</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Every English sentence must have something in the subject position. That is why we say <em><strong>It</strong> is raining outside</em> and <em><strong>There</strong> was a king</em>: <em>it</em> and <em>there</em> fill the slot. The only exception is the <strong>imperative</strong> (an order): <em>Get out!</em> Its subject, <em>you</em>, is understood by everyone, so it is dropped. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>This is why the object moves up in a passive: the empty subject slot must be filled. The lecturer stresses that the subject and object do not simply “exchange” places; each move is a need of the structure. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"grammatical-roles-and-thematic-roles\">Grammatical roles and thematic roles</h3>\n<p>Every noun in a sentence has two kinds of role. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Grammatical relations</strong>: subject, direct object, indirect object. The subject is usually the agent, but there can be non-agentive subjects as well.</li>\n<li><strong>Thematic (logical, semantic) roles</strong>: what the noun does in the event:\n<ul>\n<li><strong>agent</strong>: the one who does the action;</li>\n<li><strong>patient</strong>: the one who undergoes the effect of the action;</li>\n<li><strong>recipient</strong>: the one who receives or benefits from the action;</li>\n<li><strong>instrument</strong>: the thing used to do the action;</li>\n<li><strong>theme</strong>: the thing that is acted upon.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In an active sentence, the two kinds of role sit together. In a passive, they <strong>split</strong>: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Role</th>\n<th>Active: <em>Deepa wrote a novel.</em></th>\n<th>Passive: <em>A novel was written by Deepa.</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grammatical subject</td>\n<td>Deepa</td>\n<td>a novel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agent (logical subject)</td>\n<td>Deepa</td>\n<td>Deepa (inside <em>by Deepa</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Theme</td>\n<td>a novel (direct object)</td>\n<td>a novel (still the theme)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tense and agreement on</td>\n<td>wrote</td>\n<td>was</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So in the passive, <em>a novel</em> is the <strong>grammatical subject</strong> but not the <strong>logical subject</strong>: thematically it is still the thing written. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Be or get</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both make passives: <em>The glass <strong>was</strong> broken.</em> / <em>The glass <strong>got</strong> broken.</em> The main verb is always in its third form. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"only-transitive-verbs-have-a-passive\">Only transitive verbs have a passive</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Transitive and intransitive verbs</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>transitive</strong> verb must take a noun phrase as its object; without it the sense is incomplete. Test: ask “verb + what?”. <em>Ravi writes</em>… what? <em>A book, a letter, a novel</em>: <strong>write</strong> is transitive. An <strong>intransitive</strong> verb needs no object: <em>Ravi runs very fast</em> is complete, and <em>very fast</em> is an adverb of manner, not a noun phrase. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1470s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:30</a></p>\n<p><strong>Only transitive verbs can be made passive.</strong> <em>Ravi runs very fast</em> has no passive. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1560s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 26:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>A <strong>ditransitive</strong> verb takes <strong>two</strong> objects, and <em>give</em> is one. In <em>Ravi gave Deepa a book</em>: Ravi gave <strong>what</strong>? <em>A book</em>, the <strong>direct object</strong> (DO). <strong>To whom</strong>? <em>Deepa</em>, the <strong>indirect object</strong> (IO), the recipient. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1410s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"passives-with-two-objects\">Passives with two objects</h3>\n<p>The subject position has room for one noun. Which object moves up depends on the word order of the active sentence. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Active</th>\n<th>Order after the verb</th>\n<th>Passive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ravi gave <strong>Deepa</strong> a book.</td>\n<td>IO, then DO</td>\n<td><strong>Deepa</strong> was given a book (by Ravi).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ravi gave <strong>a book</strong> to Deepa.</td>\n<td>DO, then <em>to</em> + IO</td>\n<td><strong>A book</strong> was given to Deepa (by Ravi).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>In the first passive, the indirect object has been <strong>promoted</strong> (moved up to subject) and the direct object is left in place. In the second, the direct object is promoted.</p>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Restrictions on promotion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The indirect object moves up only from the order <em>gave Deepa a book</em>. The direct object moves up only from the order <em>gave a book to Deepa</em>. Mixing the two gives wrong sentences: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1890s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 31:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wrong: <em>A book was given Deepa.</em></li>\n<li>Wrong: <em>Deepa was given a book to.</em></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"passives-in-any-tense\">Passives in any tense</h3>\n<p>Any transitive verb, in any tense, can be made passive by the same rule. The lecture’s example: <em>She <strong>has written</strong> a note</em> → <em>A note <strong>has been written</strong> (by her)</em>. The active’s tense stays; it moves to <em>be</em>, and the main verb becomes V3. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1920s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 32:00</a></p>\n<p>The table applies this rule to the lecture’s sentence in each tense. The rule is the lecture’s; the rows marked “lecture” are its own sentences, and the rest are ours.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tense and aspect</th>\n<th>Active</th>\n<th>Passive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Present indefinite</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>writes</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>is written</strong> by Deepa.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past indefinite</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>wrote</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>was written</strong> by Deepa. (lecture)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future indefinite</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>will write</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>will be written</strong> by Deepa.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present continuous</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>is writing</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>is being written</strong> by Deepa.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past continuous</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>was writing</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>was being written</strong> by Deepa.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Present perfect</td>\n<td>She <strong>has written</strong> a note.</td>\n<td>A note <strong>has been written</strong> (by her). (lecture)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past perfect</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>had written</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>had been written</strong> by Deepa.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Future perfect</td>\n<td>Deepa <strong>will have written</strong> a novel.</td>\n<td>A novel <strong>will have been written</strong> by Deepa.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The future continuous and the three perfect continuous forms are almost never used in the passive.</p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Make a passive in three moves</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Object up</strong>: the object becomes the new subject.</li>\n<li><strong>be + V3</strong>: put <em>be</em> in the active’s tense, then the past participle (<em>wrote</em> → <em>was written</em>; <em>is writing</em> → <em>is being written</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>by + doer</strong>: add the agent with <em>by</em>, or leave it out.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Common slips</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the tense: <em>Deepa <strong>wrote</strong></em> (past) → <em>A novel <strong>was</strong> written</em>, not <em>is written</em>.</li>\n<li>Use V3, not the past form: <em>was <strong>written</strong></em>, never <em>was wrote</em>.</li>\n<li><em>Be</em> agrees with the new subject: <em>A novel <strong>was</strong> written</em>, but <em>Novels <strong>were</strong> written</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"why-we-use-the-passive\">Why we use the passive</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In an active sentence, the agent is <strong>declared</strong>: <em>I broke the glass</em> (I accept it). In a passive, the agent is <strong>suppressed</strong>: <em>The glass got broken</em> puts the event in front and hides who did it. In the same way, <em>I fired a bullet</em> is a confession that can be punished in court, while <em>A bullet was fired</em> hides the shooter. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=2010s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Contexts where the passive is preferred (the slide quotes <em>Garner’s Modern English Usage</em>, 2016): <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=1980s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 33:00</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li>When the actor is unimportant.</li>\n<li>When the actor is unknown.</li>\n<li>When you want to hide the actor’s identity.</li>\n<li>When you need to put the punch word at the end of the sentence.</li>\n<li>When the focus of the sentence is on the thing being acted on.</li>\n<li>When the passive simply sounds better.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecture’s examples, function by function: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=2250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 37:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Emphasise the receiver of the action</td>\n<td>America was discovered by Columbus.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The agent is unknown</td>\n<td>Some rare artefacts were stolen from the museum. / My watch was stolen.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The agent is not important; the action is</td>\n<td>The bridge is being repaired.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The agent is known and predictable</td>\n<td>The workers are paid weekly. (by the employer, of course)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General statements, announcements, appeals</td>\n<td>People are requested to donate generously to the relief funds.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Avoid or suppress responsibility</td>\n<td>The bullet got fired. / The bullet was fired.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hide who did it</td>\n<td>The glass got broken.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Extra emphasis on the agent</td>\n<td>The patient was killed by the doctor.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The punch word at the end</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Moving the agent out of its normal position to the end, after <em>by</em>, can give it <strong>extra emphasis</strong>: in <em>The patient was killed <strong>by the doctor</strong></em>, the shock comes in the last words. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cQ-wetyKv3U&amp;t=2160s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 36:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"adjectives-some-important-concepts\">Adjectives: some important concepts</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>adjective</strong> modifies a noun: it gives details about it. <em>A man</em> → <em>a <strong>big</strong> man</em>. This lecture skips the basic definitions and types and covers points we usually overlook: how to spot adjectives, nouns that act as adjectives, where adjectives sit, and how position can change meaning. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-adjectives-tell-us\">What adjectives tell us</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Information</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>size</td>\n<td>He is a <strong>big</strong> man.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>shape</td>\n<td>It is a <strong>round</strong> cup.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>age</td>\n<td>It is an <strong>old</strong> house.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>colour</td>\n<td>It is a <strong>red</strong> dress.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>origin</td>\n<td>It is a <strong>Chinese</strong> saucer.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>material</td>\n<td>It is an <strong>iron</strong> bridge.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>opinion</td>\n<td>She is a <strong>noble</strong> woman.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>observation</td>\n<td>It is an <strong>abandoned</strong> house.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>purpose</td>\n<td>It is a <strong>reading</strong> hall.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><em>Chinese saucer</em> does not mean saucers are made only in China; <em>Chinese</em> tells the origin of this one. <em>Iron</em> is a noun, but in <em>iron bridge</em> it works as an adjective (material). <em>Reading</em> tells the purpose of the hall. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"spotting-adjectives-by-their-endings\">Spotting adjectives by their endings</h3>\n<p>Many adjectives have typical endings, called <strong>suffixes</strong> (parts added at the end of a word). The list gives clues, not a complete rule. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ending</th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>-able / -ible</td>\n<td>uncomfortable, invisible, responsible, adorable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-al</td>\n<td>viral, illegal, educational, gradual, critical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-an</td>\n<td>Indian, American, Mexican, urban</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ar</td>\n<td>popular, spectacular, vulgar</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ent</td>\n<td>competent, patient, intelligent, silent, violent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ful</td>\n<td>shameful, powerful, harmful, thoughtful, beautiful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ic / -ical</td>\n<td>synthetic, problematic, athletic, energetic, magical, scientific</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ine</td>\n<td>feminine, masculine, bovine (of cows), canine (of dogs), equine (of horses)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ile</td>\n<td>fragile, agile, docile, fertile, virile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ive</td>\n<td>selective, predictive, informative, native, talkative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-less</td>\n<td>harmless, careless, endless, homeless, senseless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-ous</td>\n<td>precious, delicious, notorious, cautious, dangerous</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>-some</td>\n<td>tiresome, awesome, handsome, lonesome, wholesome</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Endings are clues; position decides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Patient</em> ends in -ent and is an adjective in <em>Be patient</em>. But in lecture 2.3’s <em>The <strong>patient</strong> was killed by the doctor</em>, it is a noun. Check where the word sits and what it does, not only its ending. The lecture says the same: the position of a word also tells you that it is an adjective. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"nouns-that-act-as-adjectives\">Nouns that act as adjectives</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Noun modifier</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>When two nouns stand together, the <strong>first noun can act as an adjective</strong> for the second: it tells its purpose or type. It is not usually an adjective, but it acts as one in that situation. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>dinner</strong> table (a table for dinner)</li>\n<li><strong>music</strong> class (the type of class)</li>\n<li><strong>study</strong> room</li>\n<li><strong>cricket</strong> player</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>An adjective can come before such a pair, so one noun gets <strong>two layers</strong> of description. Adjectives come <strong>before</strong> noun modifiers. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>He has an expensive music system.</th>\n<th>That is a high performance machine.</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Adjective</td>\n<td>expensive</td>\n<td>high</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noun modifier</td>\n<td>music</td>\n<td>performance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main noun</td>\n<td>system</td>\n<td>machine</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>An</em> comes before <em>expensive</em> because <em>expensive</em> begins with a vowel sound.)</p>\n<h3 id=\"attributive-and-predicative-adjectives\">Attributive and predicative adjectives</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Attributive adjective</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An adjective that goes <strong>before the noun</strong>. It attributes (gives) a particular detail to the noun that follows. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This is a <strong>wonderful / effective / great</strong> proposal.</li>\n<li>You are an <strong>honest</strong> person.</li>\n<li>I have an <strong>old</strong> car.</li>\n<li>This is a <strong>big</strong> book.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Predicative adjective</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An adjective that comes <strong>after a linking verb</strong> such as <em>be, look, become, seem</em>, in the <strong>predicate</strong>. It still describes the subject. It acts as a <strong>complement</strong>: it completes the sense, and without it the sentence is incomplete. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>This person looks <strong>suspicious</strong>.</li>\n<li>This place seems <strong>unsafe</strong>.</li>\n<li>She is <strong>beautiful</strong>.</li>\n<li>This book is <strong>big</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Attributive = attached; predicative = in the predicate</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>A</strong>ttributive adjectives are <strong>a</strong>ttached to the noun (<em>a big book</em>). <strong>P</strong>redicative adjectives sit in the <strong>p</strong>redicate, after the verb (<em>this book is big</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"same-adjective-different-meaning\">Same adjective, different meaning</h3>\n<p>Some adjectives can be used both ways, but the meaning changes with the position. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Attributive (before the noun)</th>\n<th>Predicative (after the verb)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>This <strong>particular</strong> work is so demanding that we need to give it some extra time. (<em>particular</em> = this specific one, not any other)</td>\n<td>My father is very <strong>particular</strong>. He does not approve my coming home late. (<em>particular</em> = not easy to persuade; accepts nothing else)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Her <strong>late</strong> husband left huge wealth after him. (<em>late</em> = dead, deceased)</td>\n<td>Some of you are always <strong>late</strong> in your class. (<em>late</em> = not on time)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>There are <strong>certain</strong> reasons for this delay in the project. (<em>certain</em> = some, but not specific ones)</td>\n<td>I was <strong>certain</strong> that you would succeed in your efforts. (<em>certain</em> = sure, definite)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<h3 id=\"where-adjectives-sit-in-a-phrase\">Where adjectives sit in a phrase</h3>\n<p>The lecture’s “ordering of adjectives in a phrase” lists the positions that tell you a word is an adjective: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 25:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Position</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Just before a noun</td>\n<td><strong>red</strong> dress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Between an article (<em>a, an, the</em>) and a noun</td>\n<td>the <strong>dirty</strong> room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Between a possessive (<em>his, Ram’s</em>) and a noun</td>\n<td>his <strong>big</strong> office; Ram’s <strong>white</strong> shoes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Between a demonstrative (<em>this, that, these, those</em>) and a noun</td>\n<td>that <strong>aggressive</strong> moment</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Between an amount (<em>some, most, all, a few</em>) and a noun</td>\n<td>a few <strong>ordinary</strong> things</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adjectives as complements</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>complement</strong> completes the sense of a sentence. Adjectives after a linking verb are complements (but not every complement is an adjective). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1620s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 27:00</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>She is <strong>intelligent</strong>.</li>\n<li>He is <strong>sharp, witty</strong> and <strong>responsive</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Complements come back in <a href=\"#/english2/w03\">Week 3</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"articles-and-degree-adverbs-before-an-adjective\">Articles and degree adverbs before an adjective</h3>\n<p>A <strong>degree adverb</strong> is a word like <em>very</em> that sets how strong an adjective is (more in the next section). When one joins an adjective before a noun, the order is fixed: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1680s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Indefinite article</th>\n<th>Degree adverb</th>\n<th>Adjective</th>\n<th>Noun</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>a / an</td>\n<td>fairly, very, moderately, extremely, absolutely</td>\n<td>cold</td>\n<td>day</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So: <em>a <strong>very</strong> cold day</em>, <em>an <strong>extremely</strong> cold day</em>. The article comes first; <em>very a cold day</em> and <em>fairly a cold day</em> are wrong. (The slide also lists <em>absolutely</em>; lecture 2.5 adds that <em>absolutely</em> fits best with non-gradable adjectives, so <em>an extremely cold day</em> is the safer choice.)</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quite and rather: the unusual ones</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>quite an</strong> expensive item (<em>a quite expensive item</em> is possible in writing, but it is not as common);</li>\n<li>a <strong>rather</strong> painful decision (<em>rather a painful decision</em> is possible, but it is not as common). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hkiHY0chmvk&amp;t=1710s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 28:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Several adjectives before one noun (not in this lecture)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s “ordering” is about positions, not about the order of several adjectives together. For that, grammar books give the usual order: opinion, size, age, shape, colour, origin, material, purpose, then the noun. An example of ours with the lecture’s words: <em>a <strong>big old red Chinese</strong> saucer</em> (size, age, colour, origin).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"degree-adverbs\">Degree adverbs</h2>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Degree adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>degree adverb</strong> is used before an adjective, a verb or another adverb to give information about the <strong>extent or degree</strong> of something. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Without</th>\n<th>With a degree adverb</th>\n<th>What changes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>They are sad.</td>\n<td>They are <strong>extremely</strong> sad.</td>\n<td>a greater degree of sadness</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I hate the smell of cigars.</td>\n<td>I <strong>really</strong> hate the smell of cigars.</td>\n<td>a greater degree of hatred</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>She is always late.</td>\n<td>She is <strong>almost</strong> always late.</td>\n<td>a little less than “always”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Other degree adverbs: <em>completely, fairly, quite, rather, slightly, too, totally, very (much)</em>. <em>He was <strong>completely</strong> blank. It was <strong>fairly</strong> good. It was <strong>quite</strong> dark.</em> <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"very-and-too\">Very and too</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Very and too</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Before an adjective or an adverb, <strong>very</strong> means “<strong>a high degree</strong>”; <strong>too</strong> means “<strong>more than enough</strong>”, more than is wanted or needed. So <em>too</em> is stronger than <em>very</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The weather was <strong>very</strong> hot in Chennai. Perfect for swimming. (not <em>too</em> hot: the heat is welcome)</li>\n<li>It is <strong>too</strong> hot to stay in this room – let’s find somewhere cooler. (not <em>very</em> hot: the heat is unbearable)</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Too can mean very in informal negatives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>In informal spoken English, especially in negative sentences, <em>too</em> can mean roughly the same as <em>very</em>: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>I am not <strong>too / very</strong> bothered about who fails.</li>\n<li>It is not <strong>too / very</strong> warm today, is it?</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"very-and-very-much\">Very and very much</h3>\n<p><em>Very</em> and <em>very much</em> can swap in some sentences, but not in all. What decides is the word that follows: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Before…</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>some verbs about feelings: <em>agree, doubt, fear, hope, like, want</em></td>\n<td><strong>very much</strong>, never <em>very</em></td>\n<td>I <strong>very much</strong> agree with your decision. (wrong: <em>I very agree</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>admire, appreciate, enjoy, regret</em></td>\n<td><strong>very much</strong> or <strong>much</strong>, never <em>very</em></td>\n<td>We <strong>very much</strong> enjoyed having you stay with us. / I <strong>much</strong> enjoyed the program. (wrong: <em>I very enjoyed</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>participle adjectives (-ing, -ed)</td>\n<td><strong>very</strong>, not <em>very much</em></td>\n<td>She was <strong>very</strong> disturbed to hear the news. / It is <strong>very</strong> disappointing.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a past participle that is part of a passive</td>\n<td><strong>very much</strong>, not <em>very</em></td>\n<td>The new highway was <strong>very much</strong> needed. (wrong: <em>was very needed</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Very much</em> goes only with <strong>some</strong> verbs: <em>I very much ate pizza</em> makes no sense. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></li>\n<li>A <strong>participle adjective</strong> is an adjective made from a verb, usually ending in <strong>-ing</strong> or <strong>-ed</strong> (<em>disturbed, disappointing</em>). The lecture sets reading more about them as homework. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a></li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Very or very much?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Very</em> + an adjective, including -ing/-ed adjectives (<em>very disappointing</em>). <em>Very much</em> + a feeling verb (<em>very much agree</em>) or + a passive participle (<em>was very much needed</em>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"gradable-and-non-gradable-adjectives\">Gradable and non-gradable adjectives</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gradable and non-gradable adjectives</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>gradable</strong> adjective can have <strong>different levels</strong> of its quality; modifiers make it weaker or stronger: <em>cold</em> → <em>a bit cold, very cold, extremely cold</em>. Most adjectives are gradable. A <strong>non-gradable</strong> adjective has no levels: in <em>a <strong>finished</strong> project</em>, <em>finished</em> means done; there is no “a bit finished”. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Rule: use <strong>extremely, very</strong> with gradable adjectives, and <strong>absolutely, completely</strong> with non-gradable adjectives. Words that naturally go together like this are called <strong>collocations</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a></p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Degree adverb</th>\n<th>Goes with</th>\n<th>The lecture’s examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>extremely</td>\n<td>gradable adjectives</td>\n<td>extremely effective, extremely difficult, extremely hard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>hugely</td>\n<td>gradable adjectives</td>\n<td>hugely entertaining, hugely successful</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>absolutely</td>\n<td>non-gradable adjectives</td>\n<td>absolutely clear, absolutely sure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>simply</td>\n<td>non-gradable adjectives</td>\n<td>simply awful, simply terrible</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>So say <em>the movie was <strong>hugely</strong> successful</em> rather than <em>absolutely successful</em>, and <em>it is <strong>extremely</strong> hard</em> rather than <em>simply hard</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gradable or not? The “a bit” test</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If “a bit ___” makes sense (<em>a bit cold</em>), the adjective is gradable: use <em>very</em> or <em>extremely</em>. If it does not (<em>a bit finished</em>), it is non-gradable: use <em>absolutely</em> or <em>completely</em>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"quite-two-meanings\">Quite: two meanings</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Quite</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Quite</strong> has two meanings: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uauQ5eO0Asg&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>to a particular degree, but not “very”</strong> (= fairly), with gradable adjectives: <em>I was <strong>quite</strong> satisfied with the result.</em> (fairly satisfied)</li>\n<li><strong>to a large degree, “very much”</strong> (= completely), with non-gradable adjectives: <em>No, you are <strong>quite</strong> wrong.</em> (completely wrong) / <em>“Lena is not coming until tomorrow.” Are you <strong>quite</strong> certain?</em> (completely sure)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"comment-viewpoint-and-focus-adverbs\">Comment, viewpoint and focus adverbs</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Adverbs give extra information about the verb. In <em>John reads a book <strong>in the night</strong> <strong>with his friends</strong> <strong>after dinner</strong></em>, every added phrase tells us about <em>reading</em>: when, with whom, after what. That is why the lecture calls the verb the <strong>powerhouse</strong> of the sentence and the most important part of the predicate, and why prepositional phrases are <strong>adverbial</strong> (they work as adverbs). <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The lecturer adds why this matters: <strong>accuracy is the foundation of confidence</strong>, and accuracy comes from seeing how our mind processes the structure of a sentence, not from memorising or from practice alone. Language skill has three parts: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>linguistic competence</strong>: knowing the structure of the language;</li>\n<li><strong>communicative competence</strong>: being able to use the language to communicate with others;</li>\n<li><strong>pragmatic competence</strong>: understanding the context in which we use language.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The adverbs in this lecture come from the <strong>pragmatic</strong> side: they add the speaker’s comment, angle and focus, and make speech and writing sharper. The lecture covers three types: <strong>comment</strong>, <strong>viewpoint</strong> and <strong>focus</strong> adverbs. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"comment-adverbs\">Comment adverbs</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Comment adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>comment adverb</strong> adds the speaker’s comment on what is said. It can stand <strong>at the front, in the middle or at the end</strong> of a sentence. When it applies to the <strong>whole sentence</strong>, we usually separate it from the rest with a <strong>comma</strong>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What it shows</th>\n<th>Examples</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>likelihood (how likely something is)</td>\n<td>apparently, certainly, clearly, definitely, in theory, obviously, presumably, undoubtedly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>our attitude or opinion</td>\n<td>astonishingly, frankly, generally, honestly, to be honest, interestingly, luckily, naturally, in my opinion, personally, sadly, seriously, surprisingly, unbelievably</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>our judgement of someone’s action</td>\n<td>bravely, carelessly, foolishly, generously, kindly, rightly, stupidly, wisely, wrongly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The lecture’s examples: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li>The plane <strong>apparently</strong> overshot the runway after landing. (a comment, not a certainty; in the middle)</li>\n<li>Your plan sounds fine <strong>in theory</strong>, but will it work?</li>\n<li><strong>Presumably</strong>, he speaks Spanish. (not sure; at the front, so a comma)</li>\n<li><strong>In my opinion</strong>, he is one of the best students in the class.</li>\n<li><strong>Honestly speaking</strong>, I don’t care what the world thinks.</li>\n<li>She <strong>obviously</strong> wants you to stay.</li>\n<li>Please give <strong>generously</strong> to the Blue Cross.</li>\n<li><strong>Frankly</strong>, I’m not surprised her family disowned her.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The lecture’s homework: make three or four sentences of your own with each of these adverbs, about things you really want to say, and notice them when other people speak. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"viewpoint-adverbs\">Viewpoint adverbs</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Viewpoint adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>viewpoint adverb</strong> shows the <strong>perspective</strong> (the angle) from which the speaker looks at something, to make the point clearer. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Financially</strong>, the hospitalisation has been a disaster for my neighbour. (a disaster from the money side)</li>\n<li>The sisters might be alike <strong>physically</strong>, but they have very different personalities.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<p>Other viewpoint adverbs: <em>biologically, chemically, environmentally, ideologically, logically, morally, outwardly, politically, technically, visually</em>. Phrases do the same job: <em>morally speaking, in political terms, from a technical point of view, as far as environment is concerned</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a></p>\n<p>More of the lecture’s examples: <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a></p>\n<ol>\n<li>What you did was not illegal, but it was <strong>morally</strong> wrong.</li>\n<li>Much of our behaviour is <strong>biologically</strong> determined.</li>\n<li><strong>Outwardly</strong> the couple appeared happy.</li>\n<li><strong>Technically</strong>, the two countries are still at war.</li>\n<li>He <strong>certainly</strong> was not <strong>politically</strong> inactive. (<em>certainly</em> is a comment adverb, <em>politically</em> a viewpoint adverb)</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"focus-adverbs-only-even-alone\">Focus adverbs: only, even, alone</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Focus adverb</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>focus adverb</strong> puts <strong>one part</strong> of the sentence in focus: the phrase in its scope, usually the phrase right after it. <em>Even</em> and <em>only</em> usually go in the <strong>middle</strong> of the sentence; if they refer to the <strong>subject</strong>, they may come <strong>before</strong> it. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Sentence</th>\n<th>In focus</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>My brother has <strong>only</strong> brought some books.</td>\n<td>brought some books</td>\n<td>He did not get anything other than books.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Only</strong> my brother has brought some food.</td>\n<td>my brother</td>\n<td>It is my brother and nobody else.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Even</strong> Liya can speak English.</td>\n<td>Liya</td>\n<td>You might not expect her to.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liya can <strong>even</strong> speak German.</td>\n<td>speak German</td>\n<td>In addition to everything else she can do.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>It is not possible to pass this exam with luck <strong>alone</strong>.</td>\n<td>luck</td>\n<td>Luck by itself is not enough; you need to work hard.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>When <strong>alone</strong> means <em>only</em>, it comes <strong>after</strong> the noun: <em>with luck alone</em> = <em>only with luck</em>. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a></p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Indian English</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer points out that the use and position of <em>only</em> and <em>even</em> in Indian English differ from American or British English, and he describes them from the Indian English side: their effect is local, on the phrase right after them. Pay attention to where you place them. <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Jyv6b3LU_pE&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a></p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sentence adverbs and comment adverbs overlap</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Apparently, certainly</em> and <em>obviously</em> appear in lecture 2.1 as <strong>sentence adverbs</strong> and in lecture 2.6 as <strong>comment adverbs</strong> (likelihood). Both names describe the same job: the speaker’s comment on the whole sentence. Also, words such as <em>generously</em> and <em>wisely</em> can be <strong>manner</strong> adverbs; lecture 2.6 lists them as comment adverbs that show our <strong>judgement</strong> of someone’s action.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Adverbs</strong>: manner (how?), degree (how much?), place/direction (where?), time/frequency (when? how often?), sentence adverbs (whole sentence, at the front), conjunctive adverbs (connectors: <em>nonetheless, however, consequently</em>). An adverb clause has its own subject and verb; a phrase does not.</li>\n<li><strong>Tense</strong> = time, carried by the first auxiliary or the verb form. <strong>Aspect</strong> = state: indefinite (habit), continuous (<em>be</em> + -ing), perfect (<em>have</em> + V3), perfect continuous (<em>have been</em> + -ing). 3 × 4 = 12 forms; no aspect without a verb. The -s of <em>he lives</em> is agreement, not tense.</li>\n<li><strong>Verb forms</strong>: regular verbs add -d/-ed; irregular verbs change inside (<em>begin, began, begun</em>), not at all (<em>cut</em>), or become a new word (<em>go, went</em>: suppletion).</li>\n<li><strong>Passive</strong> = <em>be</em> (or <em>get</em>) + V3 of a <strong>transitive</strong> verb. The object moves up, the agent goes into an optional <em>by</em>-phrase, and the tense stays on <em>be</em>. Two objects: <em>Deepa was given a book</em> / <em>A book was given to Deepa</em>. Use it when the agent is unknown, unimportant, obvious or hidden; to stress the receiver; for appeals; to avoid responsibility.</li>\n<li><strong>Adjectives</strong>: attributive (before the noun) vs predicative (after a linking verb); <em>particular, late, certain</em> change meaning. Clues: endings (<em>-able, -ful, -less, -ous</em>…) and positions (after an article, possessive, demonstrative or amount). Orders: <em>an expensive music system</em>; <em>a very cold day</em>; <em>quite an expensive item</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>Degree adverbs</strong>: <em>very</em> = high degree, <em>too</em> = more than needed; <em>very</em> + adjective, <em>very much</em> + feeling verb or passive participle; <em>extremely/hugely</em> + gradable, <em>absolutely/simply</em> + non-gradable; <em>quite</em> = fairly or completely.</li>\n<li><strong>Comment</strong> (likelihood, attitude, judgement), <strong>viewpoint</strong> (<em>financially, technically</em>) and <strong>focus</strong> adverbs (<em>only, even, alone</em>: the focus falls on the phrase in their scope).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What kind of adverb is each word in italics? (1) We should distribute the workload <em>equally</em>. (2) The policeman examined the documents <em>thoroughly</em>. (3) Maya is going <em>to college</em>. (4) I go to the theatre <em>occasionally</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Manner</strong>: it answers “How should we distribute it?”</li>\n<li><strong>Degree</strong>: it answers “How much (how fully) did he examine them?” The -ly ending does not make it a manner adverb.</li>\n<li><strong>Place / direction</strong>: it answers “Where is Maya going?” A prepositional phrase can work as an adverb.</li>\n<li><strong>Time / frequency</strong>: it answers “How often do I go?”</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What kind of adverb is the word in italics? (1) <em>Apparently</em>, the days are getting hotter. (2) We are still not sure, <em>however</em>, if the opportunity will come.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Sentence adverb</strong>: it is fronted, followed by a comma, and covers the whole sentence; it shows the speaker’s point of view.</li>\n<li><strong>Conjunctive adverb</strong> (connector): it links this idea to what came before and shows a contrast between them.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Adverb clause or adverb phrase? (1) He carried the load <em>until he was tired</em>. (2) You are irritated <em>without any reason</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Adverb clause</strong>: it has its own subject (<em>he</em>) and verb (<em>was</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Adverb phrase</strong>: it has no subject and verb of its own; it tells us about <em>irritated</em>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the tense and the aspect: (1) The girls <em>were playing</em> in the ground. (2) I <em>will have typed</em> a mail. (3) The girls <em>had been playing</em> effectively. (4) We <em>shall meet</em> tomorrow. (5) I <em>have typed</em> a mail now.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Past continuous</strong>: <em>were</em> (past) + <em>playing</em> (-ing = continuous).</li>\n<li><strong>Future perfect</strong>: <em>will</em> (future) + <em>have typed</em> (have + V3 = perfect).</li>\n<li><strong>Past perfect continuous</strong>: <em>had</em> (past) + <em>been</em> (perfect) + <em>playing</em> (continuous).</li>\n<li><strong>Future indefinite</strong>: <em>shall</em> (future) + the base verb, with no aspect marker.</li>\n<li><strong>Present perfect</strong>: <em>have</em> is present, and <em>have</em> + <em>typed</em> is perfect. It is present tense, not past.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In <em>He lives in Chennai</em>, is the -s a tense marker?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>No. Compare <em>I live, you live, they live</em>: no marker at all. The -s appears only with a third person singular subject (<em>he, she, it</em>), so it is an <strong>agreement</strong> marker. The present indefinite has no marker for tense or aspect; the lecture writes this as ∅ (zero).</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Give the past and the past participle of <em>go, begin, cut, write</em>, and name the kind of change.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>go</em>, <strong>went</strong>, <strong>gone</strong>: a completely new word (suppletion).</li>\n<li><em>begin</em>, <strong>began</strong>, <strong>begun</strong>: a change inside the word.</li>\n<li><em>cut</em>, <strong>cut</strong>, <strong>cut</strong>: no change.</li>\n<li><em>write</em>, <strong>wrote</strong>, <strong>written</strong>: a change inside the word (and <em>-en</em> at the end).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is the difference between tense and aspect? (the first question on the lecture’s last slide)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><strong>Tense</strong> tells the <strong>time</strong> of the action: present, past or future. It is carried by an auxiliary (<em>is, was, will</em>) or by the verb form (<em>see, saw</em>). <strong>Aspect</strong> tells the <strong>state</strong> of the event: a habit (indefinite), in progress (continuous, -ing), complete (perfect, have + V3) or complete after going on (perfect continuous). Tense can come without aspect (<em>I am tired</em>), but aspect needs an event, so there is no aspect without a verb.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Turn into the passive: (1) <em>She has written a note.</em> (2) <em>Columbus discovered America.</em> (3) <em>Engineers are repairing the bridge.</em> (4) <em>Deepa will write a novel.</em></span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>A note <strong>has been written</strong> (by her). Present perfect: <em>has</em> + <em>been</em> + V3.</li>\n<li>America <strong>was discovered</strong> by Columbus. Past: <em>was</em> + V3. The passive puts the receiver, America, first.</li>\n<li>The bridge <strong>is being repaired</strong> (by engineers). Present continuous: <em>is being</em> + V3. The agent is usually dropped, because it is not important.</li>\n<li>A novel <strong>will be written</strong> by Deepa. Future: <em>will be</em> + V3.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Make passives of <em>Ravi gave Deepa a book</em> and <em>Ravi gave a book to Deepa</em>. Then judge: <em>A book was given Deepa</em>; <em>Deepa was given a book to</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><em>Ravi gave Deepa a book</em> (indirect object first) → <strong>Deepa was given a book (by Ravi)</strong>. The indirect object is promoted; the direct object stays in place.</li>\n<li><em>Ravi gave a book to Deepa</em> (direct object first) → <strong>A book was given to Deepa (by Ravi)</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Both</strong> of the other sentences are wrong. Each mixes the two patterns: the indirect object moves up only from the first order, and the direct object only from the second.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Can <em>Ravi runs very fast</em> be made passive?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>No. <em>Run</em> is <strong>intransitive</strong>: there is no noun phrase after it (<em>very fast</em> is an adverb of manner, not an object). Only transitive verbs, which must take an object, have a passive.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In <em>A novel was written by Deepa</em>, what is the grammatical subject, and who is the agent?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>The grammatical subject is <strong>a novel</strong>: it sits in the subject position, and <em>was</em> agrees with it. But thematically it is still the <strong>theme</strong>, the thing acted upon. The <strong>agent</strong> (the logical subject) is <strong>Deepa</strong>, now inside the <em>by</em>-phrase. In the passive, grammatical roles and thematic roles split.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which function of the passive does each sentence show? (1) Some rare artefacts were stolen from the museum. (2) The workers are paid weekly. (3) People are requested to donate generously to the relief funds. (4) The bullet got fired.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>agent is unknown</strong>: nobody knows who stole them.</li>\n<li>The <strong>agent is known and predictable</strong>: the employer pays, so there is no need to say it.</li>\n<li>A <strong>general appeal or announcement</strong>: the people matter, not who is asking.</li>\n<li>To <strong>avoid responsibility</strong>: it hides who fired.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Attributive or predicative, and what does the adjective mean? (1) Her <em>late</em> husband left huge wealth after him. (2) Some of you are always <em>late</em> in your class. (3) I was <em>certain</em> that you would succeed in your efforts.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Attributive</strong> (before <em>husband</em>): <em>late</em> = dead, deceased.</li>\n<li><strong>Predicative</strong> (after <em>are</em>): <em>late</em> = not on time.</li>\n<li><strong>Predicative</strong> (after <em>was</em>): <em>certain</em> = sure, definite. (Attributive <em>certain reasons</em> would mean “some reasons, not named”.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">In <em>That is a high performance machine</em>, which word is the adjective, which is the noun modifier, and which is the main noun?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p><em>High</em> is the <strong>adjective</strong>, <em>performance</em> is a noun working as a <strong>noun modifier</strong>, and <em>machine</em> is the <strong>main noun</strong>, described by both. The adjective comes before the noun modifier.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which word is the adjective, and what tells you? (1) the dirty room (2) Ram’s white shoes (3) a few ordinary things (4) dangerous, careless, talkative</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>dirty</strong>: it sits between an article (<em>the</em>) and a noun.</li>\n<li><strong>white</strong>: it sits between a possessive (<em>Ram’s</em>) and a noun.</li>\n<li><strong>ordinary</strong>: it sits between an amount (<em>a few</em>) and a noun.</li>\n<li>All three are adjectives: <strong>-ous, -less</strong> and <strong>-ive</strong> are typical adjective endings.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Correct the order: (1) <em>very a cold day</em> (2) <em>a quite expensive item</em> (in speech)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>a very cold day</strong>: article, then degree adverb, then adjective, then noun.</li>\n<li><strong>quite an expensive item</strong>: <em>quite</em> comes before the article. (<em>A quite expensive item</em> is possible in writing, but it is less common.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>Very</em> or <em>too</em>? (1) The weather was ___ hot in Chennai. Perfect for swimming. (2) It is ___ hot to stay in this room – let’s find somewhere cooler.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>very</strong>: a high degree, and the heat is welcome.</li>\n<li><strong>too</strong>: more than wanted; the heat is unbearable, so we must leave.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\"><em>Very</em> or <em>very much</em>? (1) I ___ agree with your decision. (2) She was ___ disturbed to hear the news. (3) The new highway was ___ needed.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>very much</strong>: <em>agree</em> is a verb, and we never put <em>very</em> alone before a verb.</li>\n<li><strong>very</strong>: <em>disturbed</em> here is a participle adjective; <em>very much disturbed</em> is wrong.</li>\n<li><strong>very much</strong>: <em>needed</em> is a past participle that is part of a passive (<em>was needed</em>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Choose the better collocation: (1) the movie was <em>absolutely / hugely</em> successful (2) it is <em>extremely / simply</em> difficult (3) I am <em>absolutely / extremely</em> sure</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>hugely</strong> successful: <em>successful</em> is gradable.</li>\n<li><strong>extremely</strong> difficult: <em>difficult</em> is gradable.</li>\n<li><strong>absolutely</strong> sure: <em>sure</em> is non-gradable.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What does <em>quite</em> mean in each sentence? (1) I was quite satisfied with the result. (2) No, you are quite wrong.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fairly</strong>, to some degree but not “very”: <em>satisfied</em> is gradable.</li>\n<li><strong>Completely</strong>: <em>wrong</em> is non-gradable, and with non-gradable adjectives <em>quite</em> means “completely”.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What kind of adverb is the word in italics? (1) <em>Financially</em>, the hospitalisation has been a disaster for my neighbour. (2) <em>Frankly</em>, I’m not surprised her family disowned her. (3) The plane <em>apparently</em> overshot the runway after landing. (4) <em>Technically</em>, the two countries are still at war.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Viewpoint</strong>: from the money point of view.</li>\n<li><strong>Comment</strong> (attitude or opinion): it covers the whole sentence, so a comma follows it.</li>\n<li><strong>Comment</strong> (likelihood): it adds a comment without claiming certainty; here it sits in the middle.</li>\n<li><strong>Viewpoint</strong>: from a technical point of view.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">What is in focus? (1) My brother has <em>only</em> brought some books. (2) <em>Only</em> my brother has brought some food. (3) Liya can <em>even</em> speak German. (4) It is not possible to pass this exam with luck <em>alone</em>.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><em>brought some books</em>: he brought nothing other than books.</li>\n<li><em>my brother</em>: nobody else brought food.</li>\n<li><em>speak German</em>: in addition to everything else she can do.</li>\n<li><em>luck</em>: <em>alone</em> after a noun means <em>only</em> (= only with luck); you also need hard work.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n"});
